---
/**
 * Homepage testimonials band — a carousel mixing video and Google reviews.
 *
 * Design: the TESTIMONIALS band of `Cohen & Jaffe Homepage v1.dc.html`
 * (lines 440–512). A cream strip between the dark deadlines band and the sand
 * attorneys band: a centred head, then cards of two shapes in one row — a video
 * review with a 16:9 cover and a pull quote, and a Google review with five stars
 * and the client's own prose.
 *
 * Content comes from the `reviews` field on the `homePage` singleton. The words
 * are REAL CLIENTS' — see `scripts/seed-home-reviews.ts` for the provenance of
 * every one, and for why the artboard's rewritten versions were rejected.
 *
 * ⚠️ THIS BAND CARRIES NO DISCLAIMER OF ITS OWN, AND THAT IS A DECISION.
 * NY Rule 7.1(e)(3) requires the sentence "Prior results do not guarantee a
 * similar outcome", in those words, on any advertisement carrying a client
 * testimonial — and a band of client testimonials is squarely that. One was
 * built here and removed on the client's call (2026-09-08), because the exact
 * sentence was already printing three times on this page: the case results band
 * above, this band, and `site-footer__disclaimer`.
 *
 * What makes the removal safe is the FOOTER instance specifically — it is
 * site-wide and unconditional, so it travels with this band wherever the band
 * is reused, which the case-results one does not. `reviewsSection` therefore
 * gets NO disclaimer field when it is modelled.
 *
 * ⚠️ If the footer disclaimer is ever made conditional, shortened, or moved off
 * a page that carries reviews, this band needs its own again. That is the single
 * thing standing between these testimonials and Rule 7.1(e)(3).
 *
 * Five things are ours rather than the artboard's:
 *
 *   - THE CONTROL ROW IS NET-NEW, AND IT HAS TWO STATES. The board centres a
 *     lone "Read all 462 reviews" button and has no arrows anywhere on the page.
 *     When every review already fits there is nothing to scroll, so the arrows
 *     are absent and the button keeps the board's centred position; as soon as
 *     the track overflows the arrows appear right and the button steps left to
 *     balance them. One measurement drives both, so the button can never sit
 *     off-centre with dead space beside it. Note `.results__head-row` on the case
 *     results band uses `baseline` and says why; this row is two controls of the
 *     same height, so it centres.
 *
 *   - THE DURATION IS READ FROM THE VIDEO, NOT TYPED. It sits in the label over
 *     the cover — "VIDEO REVIEW · 2:47" — rather than as a separate eyebrow in
 *     the body, and there is no `duration` field anywhere. See `src/lib/wistia.ts`:
 *     the hand-typed "2 min" was simply wrong about a video that runs 2:47, and
 *     nobody typing it had any way to know. It is exact (`M:SS`) rather than the
 *     board's rounded "2 min", because rounding 2:47 to "3 min" is no more
 *     honest than rounding it down.
 *
 *   - NO RATING FIGURE. The board's eyebrow reads "4.9 ★ · 462 reviews" and its
 *     button "Read all 462 reviews". Both numbers are dropped: a review count
 *     hardcoded into a page goes stale the week after it ships, and a stale
 *     count on a law firm's homepage is a small credibility problem for no gain.
 *     The eyebrow is plain editorial copy over `.eyebrow--center`, which already
 *     drops the dash site-wide. The Google mark stays on the CARDS, where it
 *     identifies one review's source; over a heading introducing a MIX of video
 *     and Google reviews it would misdescribe the band.
 *
 *   - THE HEADING AND LEAD DO NOT `white-space: nowrap`. The board sets both,
 *     the same fixed-1660px trick the fee and deadlines bands carry, and at 42px
 *     the heading would force a horizontal scrollbar well above 1150px.
 *
 *   - 16px BODY COPY IS SET AT `--lh-body`, NOT THE BOARD'S 28px. The artboards'
 *     16/28, 15/26 and 19/32 leading was measured, rejected and replaced when the
 *     design system was built — it was nearly flat across the body range and
 *     INVERTED at the small end. Reintroducing 28px here would reopen it for one
 *     card. Same reason the pull quote is `--fs-24` and not the board's 25px,
 *     which maps to no step: the case results band already dropped its quote from
 *     26 to 23 after 26 crowded a narrower card than this one gets.
 *
 *   - THE BUTTON IS OSWALD, via the new global `.btn--ghost`. The board draws the
 *     gold-hairline ghost in Roboto Condensed at 54px; the typeface is the call
 *     the deadlines band already made and recorded, and 54px maps to no step, so
 *     it takes `.btn--sm` (48px) — which also makes it exactly as tall as the two
 *     arrow circles beside it, so the three controls read as one family.
 *
 * ⚠️ A REVIEW IS NEVER TRUNCATED, AND THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `line-clamp`.
 * One was written — twelve lines, meant as a runaway backstop that real copy
 * would never reach — and measurement killed it. At 1440px the four-up card is
 * 305px and Howard's 370-character review needs THIRTEEN lines, so the clamp
 * fired on the shortest real review the firm has, cutting it mid-sentence at
 * "…the staff was". The narrowest four-up card is narrower still (~272px just
 * above the 1280 breakpoint), where the same review wants about fifteen.
 *
 * Two reasons not to simply raise the number. A review is a legal testimonial
 * under Rule 7.1(e)(1), and an arbitrary machine cut can turn a qualified
 * sentence into an unqualified one — the very misrepresentation the rule is
 * about. And `line-clamp` hides text VISUALLY ONLY: the full review stays in
 * the accessible tree and the index, so a screen reader would be read a
 * different advertisement from the one a sighted visitor sees.
 *
 * So the card holds the whole review, the equal-height row is set by the
 * longest one, and the cap on length belongs in the SCHEMA as a
 * `.max(N).warning()` on `quote` — a signal the editor can see and act on,
 * rather than a silent cut nobody notices. Same house rule as every other
 * design-coupled length: warn, never error, because publishing fires the
 * deploy hook.
 */
import Star from "../assets/icons/reviews/star.svg";
import GoogleG from "../assets/icons/reviews/google-g.svg";
import { videoDuration } from "../lib/wistia";
import { urlFor, srcSet } from "../lib/image";
import type { HOME_PAGE_QUERY_RESULT } from "../sanity/sanity.types";

type Section = NonNullable<NonNullable<HOME_PAGE_QUERY_RESULT>["reviews"]>;

interface Props {
  section: Section;
}

const { section } = Astro.props;

// An empty array would otherwise draw a bare control row under nothing.
const reviews = section.reviews ?? [];

const STARS = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4];

/** The widths the 16:9 cover is served at — the card caps at 400px. */
const CARD_WIDTHS = [400, 600, 800];

/**
 * Every video's real running time, read from Wistia once per build and keyed by
 * card id. Resolved here rather than inside the map because the template cannot
 * await, and in parallel because they are independent requests. A video Wistia
 * cannot reach resolves to `null` and its card simply shows no duration.
 */
const durations = new Map(
  await Promise.all(
    reviews
      .filter((review) => review._type === "videoReview")
      .map(async (review) => [review._id, await videoDuration(review.wistiaId)] as const),
  ),
);
---

<section class="reviews section" aria-labelledby="reviews-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="reviews__head">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--center">{section.eyebrow}</p>
      <h2 class="reviews__heading" id="reviews-heading">{section.heading}</h2>
      <p class="reviews__lead">{section.lead}</p>
    </header>

    {
      reviews.length > 0 && (
        <ul class="reviews__track" id="reviews-track" role="list">
          {reviews.map((review) =>
            review._type === "videoReview" ? (
              <li class="review review--video">
                <div class="review__media">
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    class="review__play"
                    data-video-id={review.wistiaId}
                    aria-label={`Play video review: ${review.clientName}`}
                  >
                    <span class="review__play-badge" aria-hidden="true">
                      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="currentColor">
                        <path d="M7 5v14l10-7z" />
                      </svg>
                    </span>
                  </button>
                  <img
                    class="review__image"
                    src={urlFor(review.poster).width(800).url()}
                    srcset={srcSet(review.poster, CARD_WIDTHS)}
                    sizes="(max-width: 700px) 86vw, (max-width: 1024px) 50vw, (max-width: 1280px) 33vw, 25vw"
                    alt={review.poster.alt ?? ""}
                    width="800"
                    height="450"
                    loading="lazy"
                    decoding="async"
                  />
                  <span class="review__scrim" aria-hidden="true" />
                  <span class="review__kind">
                    {["Video review", durations.get(review._id)].filter(Boolean).join(" \u00b7 ")}
                  </span>
                </div>

                <figure class="review__body">
                  <blockquote class="review__quote">
                    <p>{review.headline}</p>
                  </blockquote>
                  <figcaption class="review__who">
                    {[review.clientName, review.location].filter(Boolean).join(" · ")}
                  </figcaption>
                </figure>
              </li>
            ) : (
              <li class="review review--google">
                <div class="review__head">
                  <span class="review__stars" role="img" aria-label="Five out of five stars">
                    {STARS.map(() => (
                      <Star class="review__star" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" />
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="review__source" role="img" aria-label="Google review">
                    <GoogleG class="review__g" width="20" height="20" />
                    <span class="review__source-label">Review</span>
                  </span>
                </div>

                <figure class="review__body">
                  <blockquote class="review__prose">
                    <p>{review.quote}</p>
                  </blockquote>
                  <figcaption class="review__who">
                    {[review.author, review.location].filter(Boolean).join(" · ")}
                  </figcaption>
                </figure>
              </li>
            ),
          )}
        </ul>
      )
    }

    <div class="reviews__foot">
      {/* No arrow: an arrow never goes inside a `.btn` — see global.css. */}
      {
        section.cta && (
          <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm btn--wide" href={section.cta.href}>
            {section.cta.label}
          </a>
        )
      }

      <div class="reviews__arrows" role="group" aria-label="Reviews carousel">
        <button
          type="button"
          class="reviews__arrow reviews__arrow--prev"
          aria-label="Previous review"
          aria-controls="reviews-track"
        >
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path
              d="M15 4 7 12l8 8"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="2"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
          </svg>
        </button>
        <button
          type="button"
          class="reviews__arrow reviews__arrow--next"
          aria-label="Next review"
          aria-controls="reviews-track"
        >
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path
              d="M9 4l8 8-8 8"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="2"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
          </svg>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .reviews {
    background-color: var(--cream);
  }

  /* --- Header: centred over the row --------------------------------------- */

  .reviews__head {
    text-align: center;
    margin-block-end: var(--space-44);
  }

  .reviews__heading {
    font-size: var(--fs-section);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-18);
  }

  .reviews__lead {
    font-size: var(--fs-body-lg);
    line-height: var(--lh-body-lg);
    color: var(--muted);
    /* The board runs this on one ~1100px line because it is a fixed board, and
       a centred line that long is hard to return from. `--container-prose` is
       what global.css points at for a single column of running text — there is
       no `--measure` token any more, the 600px `.prose` cap having been removed
       on the client's call when it left copy stopping short of its column. */
    max-width: var(--container-prose);
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  /* --- The track ----------------------------------------------------------
     ONE layout at every width, unlike the case results band, which is a static
     grid above 1280 and a scroller below it. Two reasons it has to be:
     the arrows are visible at desktop, so the scroller has to be live there;
     and there may be more than four reviews, which a `repeat(4, …)` grid would
     wrap onto a second row instead of scrolling. `overflow-x` on a track that
     does not overflow is inert, so desktop pays nothing for it.

     The flex basis reproduces the board's grid exactly: `repeat(4, minmax(0,
     400px))` with a 20px gap inside a 1660px container resolves to 400px
     columns, and `(100% - 3 * 20px) / 4` is the same 400px at that width. */

  .reviews__track {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-20);
    overflow-x: auto;
    /* `mandatory`, not `proximity`, and it is load-bearing: the browser has to
       rest on a snap position, so it corrects the integer rounding in the
       measured step rather than letting it accumulate over eight clicks. */
    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
    scrollbar-width: none;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-32);
    /* `overflow-x: auto` forces `overflow-y` to compute to `auto` too, so a
       focus ring on a card would be clipped or would raise a phantom vertical
       scrollbar. The same tax the case results scroller pays. */
    padding: 0 0 var(--space-8);
    list-style: none;
  }

  .reviews__track::-webkit-scrollbar {
    display: none;
  }

  .review {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    flex: 0 0 calc((100% - 3 * var(--space-20)) / 4);
    /* A flex item's `min-width: auto` resolves to min-content and can push the
       card past its basis on an unbreakable string — the attribution line is
       the exposed case, since the reset's `overflow-wrap` covers p and li but
       not figcaption. This is the flex counterpart of grid's `minmax(0, …)`. */
    min-width: 0;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
    background-color: var(--white);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* --- Card media (video reviews only) ------------------------------------ */

  .review__media {
    position: relative;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    /* `flex: none` so the cover keeps its ratio while the body stretches to
       equalise heights across the row. */
    flex: none;
    background-color: var(--border-strong);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .review__media :global(.review__image) {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 22%;
  }

  .review__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    /* The board's own scrim for this card, which is what `--scrim-bottom` was
       taken from — it carries the "VIDEO REVIEW" label at the foot. */
    background: var(--scrim-bottom);
  }

  /* The whole frame is the hit area, not the badge alone. Transparent, so the
     scrim and label still show through, and it needs an explicit z-index
     because it comes first in the DOM and would otherwise paint under the
     image. */
  .review__play {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 2;
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .review__play:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--gold);
    /* Inset, because the track clips vertical overflow. */
    outline-offset: -4px;
  }

  .review__play-badge {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: 50%;
    inset-inline-start: 50%;
    translate: -50% -50%;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 56px;
    height: 56px;
    padding-inline-start: 4px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-color: var(--gold);
    color: var(--ink);
    transition:
      background-color var(--duration) var(--ease),
      scale var(--duration) var(--ease);
  }

  .review__play:hover .review__play-badge,
  .review__play:focus-visible .review__play-badge {
    background-color: var(--gold-light);
    scale: 1.06;
  }

  .review__kind {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline-start: var(--space-18);
    inset-block-end: var(--space-14);
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    font-weight: var(--fw-bold);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    /* `--lh-label`, never `--lh-flat`: tracked uppercase micro-type wraps on a
       narrow card, and two flat-leaded lines collide. */
    line-height: var(--lh-label);
    color: var(--cream);
  }

  /* --- Card head (google reviews only) ------------------------------------ */

  .review__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-12);
    padding: var(--space-28) var(--space-28) 0;
  }

  .review__stars {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 2px;
    color: var(--gold);
  }

  .review__stars :global(.review__star) {
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    flex: none;
  }

  .review__source {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .review__source :global(.review__g) {
    flex: none;
  }

  .review__source-label {
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    line-height: var(--lh-label);
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  /* --- Card body ----------------------------------------------------------- */

  .review__body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    flex: 1;
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--space-26) var(--space-28) var(--space-28);
  }

  .review--google .review__body {
    padding-block-start: var(--space-20);
  }

  .review__quote {
    /* Same floor above the attribution's rule as the prose gets. The pull quote
       is short by design and capped in the schema, so it needs no scroll. */
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-22);
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    /* The board's 25px maps to no step. 24 rather than 26 because this card is
       narrower than the case results card, which itself dropped from 26 to 23. */
    font-size: var(--fs-24);
    font-weight: var(--fw-regular);
    line-height: var(--lh-card);
    color: var(--color-heading);
  }

  .review__prose {
    /* The gap the attribution's rule needs above it. `.review__who` pins itself
       to the foot with `margin-block-start: auto`, and auto collapses to ZERO
       once the copy fills the column — so on the longest review the last line
       sat directly on the rule. Flex margins do not collapse, so this and the
       auto add up: 22px is the floor, free space is whatever is left over. */
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-22);
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    /* `--lh-body`, NOT the board's 28px — see the docblock. */
    line-height: var(--lh-body);
    color: var(--body);

    /* ⚠️ THE CARD'S HEIGHT IS CAPPED HERE, AND THIS IS THE ONLY THING CAPPING
       IT. The row equalises to its tallest card, so one long review would grow
       all four — and the band sits above the fold on a page whose other cards
       are already sized. Thirteen lines is the height the four approved cards
       settled at, so a longer review scrolls inside its card instead of
       resizing the row.

       Expressed in lines rather than pixels: `--lh-body` is unitless (1.6) and
       `em` resolves against this element's own font-size, so `1.6 × 13em` is
       exactly thirteen line boxes at any card width and stays correct if the
       body size ever moves. A pixel value would drift from both. */
    max-height: calc(var(--lh-body) * 13 * 1em);
    overflow-y: auto;
    /* Left VISIBLE, unlike the track's, which is hidden because its arrows and
       snap points say what it does. Here the bar is the only sign that there is
       more review below the fold of the card.
       ⚠️ macOS draws overlay scrollbars that stay hidden until a scroll starts,
       so on a Mac the cue is the clipped line of text, not the bar. */
    scrollbar-width: thin;
    scrollbar-color: var(--border-deep) transparent;
  }

  .review__who {
    /* Pinned to the foot of the stretched column, so every attribution in the
       row sits on the same line whatever the copy above it does. */
    margin-block-start: auto;
    padding-block-start: var(--space-22);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-size: var(--fs-13);
    font-weight: var(--fw-bold);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    line-height: var(--lh-label);
    color: var(--gold-ink);
  }

  /* A scrollable region with nothing focusable inside it is unreachable by
     keyboard, so the script gives it a tab stop — but only while it actually
     overflows, so it is never a pointless one. */
  .review__prose:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--gold-ink);
    outline-offset: 4px;
  }

  /* --- Control row: button left, arrows right ------------------------------ */

  .reviews__foot {
    display: flex;
    /* Centre, not the case results band's baseline: those are a 42px heading
       beside a 16px link, and these are two controls of identical height. */
    align-items: center;
    /* Centred while the button is alone, which is the artboard's own layout and
       what a visitor with JS off always sees. `.is-ready` is set by the same
       measurement that reveals the arrows, so the button steps left at exactly
       the moment something appears on the right to balance it — it never sits
       off-centre with empty space beside it. */
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-24);
  }

  .reviews__foot.is-ready {
    justify-content: space-between;
  }

  /* Hidden until the script marks itself ready, which it only does when the
     track actually overflows. With four short cards at 1660px nothing scrolls
     and the arrows are ABSENT rather than dead — and with JS off they never
     appear, while the track itself still swipes. */
  .reviews__arrows {
    display: none;
    gap: var(--space-12);
  }

  .reviews__arrows.is-ready {
    display: flex;
  }

  .reviews__arrow {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* Same 48px as `.btn--sm` beside it, so the row reads as one family. */
    width: 48px;
    height: 48px;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-color: transparent;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--gold);
    color: var(--gold-ink);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition:
      background-color var(--duration) var(--ease),
      color var(--duration) var(--ease),
      opacity var(--duration) var(--ease);
  }

  .reviews__arrow:hover {
    background-color: var(--gold);
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .reviews__arrow:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--gold-ink);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* `aria-disabled`, not the `disabled` attribute, and never
     `pointer-events: none`. An arrow steps ONE card, so the last press is the
     one that disables it — a real `disabled` drops the button out of the tab
     order at that instant and strands a keyboard user on <body>. This keeps it
     focusable and announced; the handler early-returns. */
  .reviews__arrow[aria-disabled="true"] {
    opacity: 0.34;
    cursor: default;
  }

  .reviews__arrow[aria-disabled="true"]:hover {
    background-color: transparent;
    color: var(--gold-ink);
  }

  /* ---- Responsive --------------------------------------------------------
     Not from the designs — the artboard is a fixed 1660px board with no mobile
     counterpart. These blocks MUST stay after the rules they override; a media
     query carries no extra specificity, so source order is what decides. With
     four basis breakpoints this file is the one most exposed to that. */

  /* Four up would put the cards at ~272px here, too narrow for a 24px serif
     quote sitting over 16px prose. */
  @media (max-width: 1280px) {
    .review {
      flex-basis: calc((100% - 2 * var(--space-20)) / 3);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 1024px) {
    .review {
      flex-basis: calc((100% - var(--space-20)) / 2);
    }
  }

  /* The button goes full width at 768 via the global `.btn--wide` rule, so the
     row has to stack before the arrows are pushed off the end of it. */
  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .reviews__foot {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
      gap: var(--space-20);
    }

    .reviews__arrows {
      justify-content: center;
    }
  }

  /* One up with a sliver of the next — the same 86% the case results scroller
     uses, so the two bands swipe identically. */
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .review {
      flex-basis: 86%;
    }

    /* ⚠️ THE HEIGHT CAP IS LIFTED HERE, DELIBERATELY. It exists to stop one long
       review growing a ROW of four, and at one-up there is no row to protect —
       each card is alone on screen. Keeping it would buy nothing and cost a
       nested vertical scroller inside the page's own vertical scroll, which is
       the classic trap: a swipe up over the card scrolls the review instead of
       the page, and the page only moves once the review runs out. The
       horizontal track has no such conflict — different axis. */
    .review__prose {
      max-height: none;
      overflow-y: visible;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .review__play:hover .review__play-badge,
    .review__play:focus-visible .review__play-badge {
      scale: none;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  /**
   * Reviews carousel arrows.
   *
   * The scroller is pure CSS scroll-snap; this only steps it and mirrors the
   * scroll extent into the two arrows. With the script absent the band still
   * swipes and the arrows never appear — `.is-ready` is what reveals them, and
   * it is only set when the track genuinely overflows, so the arrows are never
   * dead controls.
   *
   * An arrow advances ONE CARD, and its state is keyed on the SCROLL EXTENT
   * rather than a card index. That is what makes the last step work: at an 86%
   * basis the final card can never reach the start edge, so an index-based "at
   * the end" test would be permanently false. `scrollLeft >= max - 1` is not.
   *
   * SCOPED TO EACH `.reviews` ROOT. An Astro <script> is bundled once per page
   * however many times its component renders, so the bare
   * `document.querySelector` this file could have used would wire exactly one
   * instance — which is what the case results band does, and why a second
   * results band on one page would silently have inert dots.
   */
  for (const root of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".reviews")) {
    const track = root.querySelector<HTMLUListElement>(".reviews__track");
    const arrows = root.querySelector<HTMLDivElement>(".reviews__arrows");
    const foot = root.querySelector<HTMLDivElement>(".reviews__foot");
    const prev = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>(".reviews__arrow--prev");
    const next = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>(".reviews__arrow--next");
    if (!track || !arrows || !prev || !next) continue;

    const cards = [...track.querySelectorAll<HTMLLIElement>(".review")];
    if (cards.length === 0) continue;

    const proses = [...track.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".review__prose")];

    // Held in a variable: an unreferenced MediaQueryList can be garbage
    // collected along with its listener.
    const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");

    let step = 0;
    const maxScroll = () => track.scrollWidth - track.clientWidth;

    // The PITCH — one card plus one gap — measured rather than reconstructed.
    // `offsetWidth` is integer-rounded while the basis is fractional, and
    // rebuilding it as width + gap would mean reading the gap back out of
    // getComputedStyle at every breakpoint. The delta between two `offsetLeft`
    // values IS the pitch, and since both share an offsetParent it cancels.
    const measure = () => {
      step =
        cards.length > 1 ? cards[1].offsetLeft - cards[0].offsetLeft : cards[0].offsetWidth;

      // A capped review scrolls inside its card, and a scroll box holding no
      // focusable element cannot be reached by keyboard at all. Give it a tab
      // stop only while it overflows — below 700px the cap is lifted and none
      // of them do, so the tab order stays exactly as long as it needs to be.
      for (const prose of proses) {
        if (prose.scrollHeight - prose.clientHeight > 1) {
          prose.setAttribute("tabindex", "0");
          prose.setAttribute("role", "region");
          prose.setAttribute("aria-label", "Review, scrollable");
        } else {
          prose.removeAttribute("tabindex");
          prose.removeAttribute("role");
          prose.removeAttribute("aria-label");
        }
      }
    };

    // Only write when the value actually changes — this runs once per frame,
    // and a repeated aria-disabled write makes some screen readers re-announce.
    const set = (el: Element, name: string, value: string) => {
      if (el.getAttribute(name) !== value) el.setAttribute(name, value);
    };

    // Takes a position rather than reading one, so a click can paint the end
    // state immediately instead of waiting out the smooth scroll.
    const paint = (at: number) => {
      const max = maxScroll();
      // `> 1`, not `> 0`: a fractional flex basis leaves sub-pixel slop.
      const scrollable = max > 1;
      arrows.classList.toggle("is-ready", scrollable);
      // The button is centred while it stands alone and steps left once the
      // arrows join it, so the row is never lopsided.
      foot?.classList.toggle("is-ready", scrollable);
      // A scroll container whose children hold nothing focusable — which a row
      // of Google cards is — cannot otherwise be reached by keyboard at all.
      // Only while it can actually scroll, so it is never a pointless tab stop.
      if (scrollable) set(track, "tabindex", "0");
      else track.removeAttribute("tabindex");
      set(prev, "aria-disabled", String(!scrollable || at <= 1));
      set(next, "aria-disabled", String(!scrollable || at >= max - 1));
    };

    const sync = () => paint(track.scrollLeft);

    const go = (direction: 1 | -1) => {
      if (!step) measure();
      if (!step) return;
      const target = Math.max(0, Math.min(track.scrollLeft + direction * step, maxScroll()));
      paint(target);
      track.scrollTo({
        left: target,
        // Read here rather than left to CSS. The global reduced-motion block
        // sets `scroll-behavior: auto !important`, but an explicit `behavior`
        // in the options bag beats the CSS property, so the global rule does
        // not cover a programmatic scroll.
        behavior: reduceMotion.matches ? "auto" : "smooth",
      });
    };

    // `aria-disabled` does not block activation the way `disabled` does, so the
    // handler has to.
    prev.addEventListener("click", () => {
      if (prev.getAttribute("aria-disabled") !== "true") go(-1);
    });
    next.addEventListener("click", () => {
      if (next.getAttribute("aria-disabled") !== "true") go(1);
    });

    let frame = 0;
    track.addEventListener(
      "scroll",
      () => {
        // Scroll fires far faster than paint; coalesce to one read per frame.
        cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
        frame = requestAnimationFrame(sync);
      },
      { passive: true },
    );

    // A ResizeObserver rather than a `resize` listener: it fires once on
    // observe, so it is also the initial measure; it catches the reflow when
    // the webfonts land, which `display=swap` guarantees happens after first
    // paint; and it catches container changes a window resize never reports.
    // `paint` only touches a SIBLING of the observed element, so it cannot loop.
    new ResizeObserver(() => {
      measure();
      sync();
    }).observe(track);
  }
</script>
